/tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><ul><li><blockquote><p>100 million DAU</p></bloc=
kquote></li><li><blockquote><p>Concurrent users: 10% * 100 million =3D 10 m=
illion</p></blockquote></li><li><blockquote><p>Users report their locations=
 every 30 seconds.</p></blockquote></li><li><blockquote><p>Location update =
QPS =3D 10 million / 30 =3D ~334,000</p></blockquote></li></ul></td></tr></=
tbody></table>
<h2 id=3D"step-2---propose-high-level-design-and-get-buy-in">Step 2 - Propo=
se High-Level Design and Get Buy-In</h2>
<p>In this section, we will discuss the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>High-level design</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>API design</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Data model</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>In other chapters, we usually discuss API design and data model before t=
he high-level design. However, for this problem, the communication protocol=
 between client and server might not be a straightforward HTTP protocol, as=
 we need to push location data to all friends. Without understanding the hi=
gh-level design, it=E2=80=99s difficult to know what the APIs look like. Th=
erefore, we discuss the high-level design first.</p>
<h3 id=3D"high-level-design">High-level design</h3>
<p>At a high level, this problem calls for a design with efficient message =
passing. Conceptually, a user would like to receive location updates from e=
very active friend nearby. It could in theory be done purely peer-to-peer, =
that is, a user could maintain a persistent connection to every other activ=
e friend in the vicinity (Figure 2).</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a network topology diagram showing a mesh n=
etwork of seven mobile phones. Each phone is depicted as a simple icon with=
in a circle, interconnected by lines representing communication links.  The=
 phones are not arranged in any particular geometric pattern; instead, they=
 are connected in a somewhat irregular, decentralized manner, with each pho=
ne having multiple connections to other phones in the network.  There are n=
o labels, URLs, or parameters present; the diagram solely illustrates the c=
onnectivity between the devices, highlighting the characteristic many-to-ma=
ny connections of a mesh network where any device can communicate directly =
with any other device, potentially through multiple paths." loading=3D"lazy=
" width=3D"500" height=3D"275" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"ht=
tps://bytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/nearby-friends/=
figure-2-peer-to-peer-TDFPCCFK.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><di=
v style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py=
-1">Figure 2 Peer-to-peer</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>This solution is not practical for a mobile device with sometimes flaky =
connections and a tight power consumption budget, but the idea sheds some l=
ight on the general design direction.</p>
<p>A more practical design would have a shared backend and look like this:<=
/p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a simplified system architecture diagram sh=
owing a user interacting with a backend system that communicates with three=
 other users, labeled 'Friend A,' 'Friend B,' and 'Friend C.'  The diagram =
uses a cloud shape to represent the 'Backend' server. A circle containing a=
 smartphone icon represents each user, with 'User' labeling the initiating =
user and 'Friend A,' 'Friend B,' and 'Friend C' labeling the recipient user=
s.  Arrows indicate the direction of information flow.  The 'User' sends in=
formation to the 'Backend,' which then distributes information to 'Friend A=
,' 'Friend B,' and 'Friend C.'  The diagram illustrates a one-way communica=
tion flow from the user to the backend and then to the friends, suggesting =
a broadcast or distribution mechanism." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"500" heig=
ht=3D"209" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com=
/images/courses/system-design-interview/nearby-friends/figure-3-shared-back=
end-JI6MRFAF.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display=
: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 3 Share=
d backend</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>What are the responsibilities of the backend in Figure 3?</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Receive location updates from all active users.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>For each location update, find all the active friends who should receive=
 it and forward it to those users=E2=80=99 devices.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If the distance between two users is over a certain threshold, do not fo=
rward it to the recipient=E2=80=99s device.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This sounds pretty simple. What is the issue? Well, to do this at scale =
is not easy. We have 10 million active users. With each user updating the l=
ocation information every 30 seconds, there are 334K updates per second. If=
 on average each user has 400 friends, and we further assume that roughly 1=
0% of those friends are online and nearby, every second the backend forward=
s 334K * 400 * 10% =3D 14 million location updates per second. That is a lo=
t of updates to forward.</p>
<h4 id=3D"proposed-design">Proposed design</h4>
<p>We will first come up with a high-level design for the backend at a lowe=
r scale. Later in the deep dive section, we will optimize the design for sc=
ale.</p>
<p>Figure 4 shows the basic design that should satisfy the functional requi=
rements. Let=E2=80=99s go over each component in the design.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram for a mobile =
application.  Mobile Users connect to a Load Balancer via HTTP and WebSocke=
t (WS) protocols. The Load Balancer distributes requests to either WebSocke=
t Servers or API Servers.  WebSocket Servers handle bi-directional location=
 information, communicating with a Redis Pub/Sub system and a Location Cach=
e.  The WebSocket Servers also interact directly with the Location History =
Database.  API Servers handle user management, friendship management, and a=
uthentication, interacting with the User Database.  Both the WebSocket Serv=
ers and API Servers utilize a Location Cache to improve performance.  Data =
flows between these components are indicated by arrows, showing the directi=
on of communication and the type of information exchanged (e.g., bi-directi=
onal location info).  The databases (User Database and Location History Dat=
abase) store persistent data, while the caches (Location Cache) provide fas=
ter access to frequently used information." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"650" =
height=3D"559" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego=
.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/nearby-friends/figure-4-high-le=
vel-design-35BO5JE4.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"=
display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure =
4 High-level design</figcaption></div></figure>
<h4 id=3D"load-balancer">Load balancer</h4>
<p>The load balancer sits in front of the RESTful API servers and the state=
ful, bi-directional WebSocket servers. It distributes traffic across those =
servers to spread out load evenly.</p>
<h4 id=3D"restful-api-servers">RESTful API servers</h4>
<p>This is a cluster of stateless HTTP servers that handles the typical req=
uest/response traffic. The API request flow is highlighted in Figure 5. Thi=
s API layer handles auxiliary tasks like adding/removing friends, updating =
user profiles, etc. These are very common and we will not go into more deta=
il.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram for a mobile =
application.  Mobile Users connect to a Load Balancer via HTTP and WebSocke=
t (WS) protocols, indicated by numbered arrows (1). The Load Balancer distr=
ibutes requests to either WebSocket Servers or API Servers (2).  WebSocket =
Servers handle bi-directional location information, communicating with a Re=
dis Pub/Sub system for real-time updates and accessing a Location Cache for=
 faster retrieval.  The WebSocket Servers also interact with a Location His=
tory Database.  API Servers handle user management, friendship management, =
and authentication, interacting with a User Database (3).  Both the Locatio=
n Cache and the User Database store persistent data.  The diagram illustrat=
es the flow of information and the interaction between different components=
, highlighting the use of a load balancer for scalability and caching mecha=
nisms for performance optimization." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"650" height=
=3D"599" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/i=
mages/courses/system-design-interview/nearby-friends/figure-5-restful-api-r=
equest-flow-SL4EZ27Z.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D=
"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure=
 5 RESTful API request flow</figcaption></div></figure>
<h4 id=3D"websocket-servers">Websocket servers</h4>
<p>This is a cluster of stateful servers that handles the near real-time up=
date of friends=E2=80=99 locations. Each client maintains one persistent We=
bSocket connection to one of these servers. When there is a location update=
 from a friend who is within the search radius, the update is sent on this =
connection to the client.</p>
<p>Another major responsibility of the WebSocket servers is to handle clien=
t initialization for the =E2=80=9Cnearby friends=E2=80=9D feature. It seeds=
 the mobile client with the locations of all nearby online friends. We will=
 discuss how this is done in more detail later.</p>
<p>Note =E2=80=9CWebSocket connection=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9CWebSocket conne=
ction handler=E2=80=9D are interchangeable in this chapter.</p>
<h4 id=3D"redis-location-cache">Redis location cache</h4>
<p>Redis is used to store the most recent location data for each active use=
r. There is a Time to Live (TTL) set on each entry in the cache. When the T=
TL expires, the user is no longer active and the location data is expunged =
from the cache. Every update refreshes the TTL. Other KV stores that suppor=
t TTL could also be used.</p>
<h4 id=3D"user-database">User database</h4>
<p>The user database stores user data and user friendship data. Either a re=
lational database or a NoSQL database can be used for this.</p>
<h4 id=3D"location-history-database">Location history database</h4>
<p>This database stores users=E2=80=99 historical location data. It is not =
directly related to the =E2=80=9Cnearby friends=E2=80=9D feature.</p>
<h4 id=3D"redis-pubsub-server">Redis pub/sub server</h4>
<p>Redis pub/sub [2] is a very lightweight message bus. Channels in Redis p=
ub/sub are very cheap to create. A modern Redis server with GBs of memory c=
ould hold millions of channels (also called topics). Figure 6 shows how Red=
is Pub/Sub works.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram illustrating =
a publish-subscribe (Pub/Sub) model using Redis.  The diagram is divided in=
to three sections. The leftmost section, labeled 'Users publish location up=
date,' contains two rectangular boxes representing 'Publisher 1' and 'Publi=
sher 2,' each publishing location updates.  These publishers send data to t=
he central section, labeled 'Redis Pub/Sub,' which contains two cylindrical=
 boxes representing channels: 'User 1's channel' and 'User 2's channel.'  P=
ublisher 1 sends data to 'User 1's channel,' and Publisher 2 sends data to =
'User 2's channel.' The rightmost section, labeled 'Friends,' contains thre=
e rectangular boxes representing 'Subscriber 1,' 'Subscriber 2,' and 'Subsc=
riber 3.'  'User 1's channel' sends data to 'Subscriber 1.' 'User 2's chann=
el' sends data to 'Subscriber 2' and 'Subscriber 3,' indicating that multip=
le subscribers can listen to a single channel.  Arrows depict the unidirect=
ional flow of location update data from publishers to channels and from cha=
nnels to subscribers." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"700" height=3D"308" decodi=
ng=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/s=
ystem-design-interview/nearby-friends/figure-6-redis-pub-sub-KDAMADYD.svg" =
style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-co=
ntent: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 6 Redis Pub/Sub</figcapti=
on></div></figure>
<p>In this design, location updates received via the WebSocket server are p=
ublished to the user=E2=80=99s own channel in the Redis pub/sub server. A d=
edicated WebSocket connection handler for each active friend subscribes to =
the channel. When there is a location update, the WebSocket handler functio=
n gets invoked, and for each active friend, the function recomputes the dis=
tance. If the new distance is within the search radius, the new location an=
d timestamp are sent via the WebSocket connection to the friend=E2=80=99s c=
lient. Other message buses with lightweight channels could also be used.</p=
>
<p>Now that we understand what each component does, let=E2=80=99s examine w=
hat happens when a user=E2=80=99s location changes from the system=E2=80=99=
s perspective.</p>
<h3 id=3D"periodic-location-update">Periodic location update</h3>
<p>The mobile client sends periodic location updates over the persistent We=
bSocket connection. The flow is shown in Figure 7.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram for a mobile =
application.  Mobile Users connect to a Load Balancer via HTTP and WebSocke=
t (WS) protocols. The Load Balancer distributes requests to WebSocket Serve=
rs (labeled 7) and API Servers.  WebSocket Servers handle bi-directional lo=
cation information, communicating with the Load Balancer (labeled 2) and in=
teracting with a Redis Pub/Sub system (labeled 6) and a Location Cache (lab=
eled 4).  The API Servers manage user accounts, friendships, and authentica=
tion, interacting with a User Database.  Both WebSocket Servers and API Ser=
vers access a Location History Database (labeled 3).  Numbered arrows indic=
ate the flow of information between components.  For example, arrow 1 shows=
 the connection from Mobile Users to the Load Balancer, and arrow 7 shows t=
he connection from the WebSocket Servers to the Redis Pub/Sub.  The system =
uses caching (Location Cache) to improve performance." loading=3D"lazy" wid=
th=3D"700" height=3D"594" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https:/=
/bytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/nearby-friends/figur=
e-7-periodic-location-update-KBIO53BB.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></=
div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption clas=
s=3D"py-1">Figure 7 Periodic location update</figcaption></div></figure>
<ol>
<li>
<p>The mobile client sends a location update to the load balancer.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The load balancer forwards the location update to the persistent connect=
ion on the WebSocket server for that client.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The WebSocket server saves the location data to the location history dat=
abase.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The WebSocket server updates the new location in the location cache. The=
 update refreshes the TTL. The WebSocket server also saves the new location=
 in a variable in the user=E2=80=99s WebSocket connection handler for subse=
quent distance calculations.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The WebSocket server publishes the new location to the user=E2=80=99s ch=
annel in the Redis pub/sub server. Steps 3 to 5 can be executed in parallel=
.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>When Redis pub/sub receives a location update on a channel, it broadcast=
s the update to all the subscribers (WebSocket connection handlers). In thi=
s case, the subscribers are all the online friends of the user sending the =
update. For each subscriber (i.e., for each of the user=E2=80=99s friends),=
 its WebSocket connection handler would receive the user location update.</=
p>
</li>
<li>
<p>On receiving the message, the WebSocket server, on which the connection =
handler lives, computes the distance between the user sending the new locat=
ion (the location data is in the message) and the subscriber (the location =
data is stored in a variable with the WebSocket connection handler for the =
subscriber).</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>This step is not drawn on the diagram. If the distance does not exceed t=
he search radius, the new location and the last updated timestamp are sent =
to the subscriber=E2=80=99s client. Otherwise, the update is dropped.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Since understanding this flow is extremely important, let=E2=80=99s exam=
ine it again with a concrete example, as shown in Figure 8. Before we start=
, let=E2=80=99s make a few assumptions.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>User 1=E2=80=99s friends: user 2, user 3, and user 4.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>User 5=E2=80=99s friends: user 4 and user 6.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
